Fixed #1104 and committed it as f6c18d57. The trajectory block now fits under its cap, and if it ever gets cut again, the cut lists the sections it dropped.

What changed (only scripts/extract_trajectory.py, plus the matching note in ARCHITECTURE.md):
• The cap is now worked out from the block's size instead of raised a bit at a time. TOTAL_BYTE_CAP goes from 3424 to 6144: the 5063 bytes measured on #1104, plus about 20%. TOTAL_LINE_CAP stays at 100. The old "+N pays for exactly the new section" comment is marked superseded where it stood rather than deleted. That rule only kept whatever had already been lost: four whole sections were cut every session.
• Any cut now names what it dropped. cap_output adds a line like (cut: Third, Fourth, Fifth) after the old ... (truncated to fit token budget) marker, which is unchanged. A section counts as cut if any part of it is missing, including one whose header survived but whose body didn't. Room for that line is set aside inside the budget, so the output still stays under the cap. A block that's already under the cap comes back unchanged.
• Stale sentences fixed. The two comments that said "TOTAL_BYTE_CAP is 3072" and "is 3KB" now point at the constant instead of repeating a number. ARCHITECTURE.md's "3KB, typical 2–3KB" line is marked superseded too.

Measured on the real audit log (fetched it, ran the script the way evolve.sh does, then removed the worktree): the block is 5336 bytes and 78 lines. Nothing is cut, all 13 sections are there, and ## Epistemic blind spots is 602 bytes. The block has grown about 270 bytes since #1104 was measured, so the real headroom is about 800 bytes (roughly 15%), not 20%. That's in the commit message.

Checks:
• extract_trajectory.py --test: 540 ok, 0 failures. The four new tests cover a cut in the middle of the third of five sections, the under-cap case, output size with the list, and a cut by line count alone.
• Positive control, done as one mutate→run→restore step: making the cut list empty fails exactly the four tests that check the names. Restored, all green.
• tests/harness_logic.sh: 321 passed, 0 failed.
• fmt, clippy with -D warnings, and cargo test (7065 passed, 0 failed) all pass.

One process note: my first three edits to the file ran in parallel and two were silently lost. A self-test failure showed it (the cap was still 3424), and I redid them one at a time.

Still open: nothing fails if the block grows past 6144 again. The (cut: …) line only shows the loss in the planner's prompt; it doesn't block anything.
